---
/**
 * Fairfax County Veterans Treatment Docket.
 *
 * On the legacy site this had its own top-level nav slot, labeled "Fairfax
 * Court" — which reads like a courthouse address rather than help for a
 * veteran facing charges. It also carried two children that did not belong to
 * it, including a membership invitation.
 *
 * Rewritten so the veteran's path comes first and the volunteer's second. The
 * legacy volunteer call-to-action contained three typos ("counlselling",
 * "informarion") in its only two sentences.
 */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import ContactList from '../../components/ContactList.astro';
import ReviewNote from '../../components/ReviewNote.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/CallToAction.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Veterans Treatment Court"
  description="The Fairfax County Veterans Treatment Docket offers treatment and a veteran mentor instead of a jail sentence. Chapter 227 members serve as the mentors."
  eyebrow="Get help"
  lede="If you are a veteran facing charges in Fairfax County that trace back to addiction, PTSD or a brain injury, there may be a route through the courts that ends in treatment rather than a cell."
  width="prose"
>
  <h2>What the Treatment Docket is</h2>
  <p>
    The Fairfax County Veterans Treatment Docket is a specialized court track for veterans whose offenses are tied to
    service-connected problems — substance abuse, post-traumatic stress, traumatic brain injury. Instead of the
    ordinary criminal path, a participating veteran gets a structured treatment plan, regular court supervision, and
    a mentor who served.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chapter 227 supplies those mentors. Under Don Sutherland, a chapter director, the chapter has become the leading
    source of volunteer mentors on the docket, and it funds the mentor program as a line item in its annual budget.
  </p>

  <h2>How to find out whether it applies to you</h2>
  <p>
    Eligibility is decided by the court, not by the chapter. If you or a family member is facing charges in Fairfax
    County, raise the Veterans Treatment Docket with your defense attorney, or contact the chapter and we will point
    you at the right person.
  </p>

  <ReviewNote topic="Court contact details">
    The chapter&rsquo;s pages describe the docket but never link to Fairfax County&rsquo;s own information about it,
    and never give a court contact. A veteran reading this page still cannot reach the program directly. The chapter
    should supply the current county contact and link.
  </ReviewNote>

  <h2>Volunteering as a mentor</h2>
  <p>
    The docket needs enough mentors to keep one-to-one coverage for every participant, and the program has expanded
    to a second docket covering juvenile and domestic relations cases. You do not have to live in Fairfax County to
    serve as a mentor. You do have to have served.
  </p>

  <ContactList only={['treatment-court']} />

  <h2>Watch how it works</h2>
  <p>
    Justice For Vets, the national organization behind veterans treatment courts, produced two short films — one on
    what mentors actually do, and one following a veteran through the program.
  </p>
  <ul class="videos">
    <li>
      <a href="https://youtu.be/bQYd6Zn47AQ" rel="noopener">Volunteer Mentors in Veterans Treatment Court</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://youtu.be/q984FI1tcN4" rel="noopener">Veterans Treatment Court: a success story</a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <CallToAction
    title="Mentors are always needed"
    primary={{ label: 'Contact the chapter', href: '/contact' }}
    secondary={{ label: 'Join the chapter', href: '/join' }}
  >
    Every veteran on the docket is supposed to have one mentor of their own. Keeping that ratio is the constraint.
  </CallToAction>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  h2 {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-7);
    margin-block-end: var(--s-4);
  }

  p + p {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
  }

  .videos {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-4);
    padding-inline-start: var(--s-5);
  }

  .videos li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-2);
  }
</style>
